    "summary": "Stellar Playing‑Uncle John’s‑Morning Dew sandwich, soaring HCS & tight jams",
    "blurb": "The UCLA show shines with a flawless Playing In The Band → Uncle John's Band → Morning Dew sequence and an inspired Here Comes Sunshine. Jerry’s lead work is on fire, Keith adds jazzy organ textures, and the rhythm section drives the set with crisp precision. The soundboard sources are all pristine, making this a must‑listen peak‑'73 performance.",
    "review": "On November 17, 1973 at Pauley Pavilion the Dead delivered one of their late‑’70s peaks. Set 1 opened with a bright Here Comes Sunshine that set the tone for a soaring first half, followed by classic staples like China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider and a tight Brown Eyed Women. The second set is where the magic truly unfolds: the legendary Playing In The Band sandwich (Playing → Uncle John's Band → Morning Dew → Uncle John's Band → Playing) rolls out with Jerry’s “broken‑angel” guitar soaring over Keith’s bristling organ, while Phil’s bass adds “spice” and “bombs.” Bill Kreutzmann and Mickey Hart lock in tight, driving the grooves through Eyes Of The World and a jubilant Sugar Magnolia. The encore Casey Jones caps a night that feels both relaxed and urgent, a perfect snapshot of the band at their improvisational zenith. Across all five soundboard recordings the mix is described as pristine, crisp, and balanced – the kind of tape you can hear every nuance of the jam‑sandwich and the surf‑guitar tone on HCS. For anyone hunting a definitive late‑’73 show, this UCLA gig delivers both historic set‑list flow and top‑tier audio fidelity.",
